        "math_academy_id": "q-98157",
        "problem": "Calculate ${\\int }_{0}^{2}[2f(t)+3g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t$ given that ${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\u27e8{e}^{2}-1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}\u20612,\\,3\u27e9$ and ${\\int }_{0}^{2}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\u27e83,\\,-1,\\,5\u27e9.$",
        "worked_solution": "From the vector addition and scalar multiplication rules, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}[2f(t)+3g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t=2{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+3{\\int }_{0}^{2}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[2f(t)+3g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+3{\\int }_{0}^{2}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\u27e8{e}^{2}-1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}\u20612,\\,3\u27e9+3\u27e83,\\,-1,\\,5\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82{e}^{2}-2,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}\u20612,\\,6\u27e9+\u27e89,\\,-3,\\,15\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82{e}^{2}+7,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}\u20612-3,\\,21\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
